  <section class="section" id="agents">
    <div class="shell-wide hermes-sec">
      <div class="hermes-copy">
        <div class="sec-tag">/ agents, tools + memory</div>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Agents that<br /><span class="accent">live on the box</span>.</h2>
        <p class="sec-sub">
          Hermes installs and bootstraps itself on first run, confined by its own
          systemd unit and prewired to the local <code class="mono accent-mono">/v1</code> API,
          hal0's MCP servers and memory. Reach it from Telegram or Discord; it
          chains tool calls unattended for hours and writes each run back to memory.
        </p>
        <ul class="hermes-points">
          <li>Self-bootstraps: env probe → model wiring → MCP memory → persona.</li>
          <li><code class="mono">hal0-agent@hermes.service</code>: NoNewPrivileges, ProtectSystem=strict, a pinned ReadWritePaths set, and the terminal off by default.</li>
          <li>Smart command approvals: your local <code class="mono">hal0/agent</code> model pre-screens risky shell commands and falls back to asking you. Manual mode asks every time.</li>
          <li>Gated MCP tools wait in an approval queue for a human.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="hermes-cta">
          <a href="https://forum.hal0.dev/t/agents-hal0-docs/23" class="btn">Agents &amp; memory →</a>
          <span class="hermes-hint">hover to tilt · click to flip</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hermes-card-wrap">
        <HermesCard />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="shell-wide agent-tiles">
      <article class="agent-tile">
        <div class="at-tag">hal0-brain</div>
        <h3 class="at-title">A resident operator, not a chatbot</h3>
        <p class="at-body">
          hal0's own small always-on model, wired to hal0's admin catalog and
          reachable from the dashboard's Agent Chat or{' '}
          <code class="mono">hal0 chat --brain</code> on a headless box. Ask it what's
          loaded, what the box is doing, what's eating the memory pool. It ships
          read-only: it reads and explains out of the box, and one config line
          lets it act.
        </p>
        <div class="at-foot">its own small model · <strong>no</strong> dependency on Hermes</div>
      </article>

      <article class="agent-tile">
        <div class="at-tag">mcp</div>
        <h3 class="at-title">The platform is the tool catalog</h3>
        <p class="at-body">
          Two Streamable-HTTP servers mount onto the API,
          <code class="mono">/mcp/admin</code> and <code class="mono">/mcp/memory</code>,
          so any MCP client can drive the box: models, slots, profiles, stacks,
          memory, bench, telemetry and the updater. A validator keeps the gating
          tiers from overlapping, so the catalog cannot drift quietly.
        </p>
        <div class="at-foot">destructive calls wait in an <strong>approval queue</strong> for a human</div>
      </article>

      <article class="agent-tile">
        <div class="at-tag">memory</div>
        <h3 class="at-title">Recall that reaches no one</h3>
        <p class="at-body">
          Memory runs on a Hindsight engine embedded on the box and bound to
          loopback, local embeddings included. Writes go to a shared namespace by
          default or to an agent's private one; recall merges, ranks and fits the
          result to a token budget.
        </p>
        <div class="at-foot"><strong>26</strong> memory tools · destructive ops write an attributable audit row</div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="shell-wide">
      <p class="shot-note">
        Key auth is optional and off by default, the trusted-LAN posture. Once you
        set an admin key, off-box callers need it for admin routes. hal0 has no TLS
        of its own: put your reverse proxy in front and allow its origin with
        <code class="mono">hal0 network public-url set</code>. Updates are signed, the
        agent runs in a contained unit, and destructive memory operations are audited.
      </p>
    </div>
